# PBSD build options. Include from /etc/src.conf, or make SRCCONF=...
#
# Every architecture is first class. This file used to say the opposite -
# WITHOUT_LIB32, and the LLVM backends for arm, powerpc and riscv turned off
# - which did not make the assembly problem smaller, it hid most of it. The
# tree carries 71,905 lines of its own assembly across six architectures;
# with four of them dropped it looked like 40,156.
#
# See docs/ASSEMBLY.md for the per-architecture breakdown.

# No machine-dependent assembly in libc, msun or libmd.
#
# This is upstream's own switch, not a PBSD invention. lib/libc/Makefile:159:
#
#   .if empty(MDSRCS) || ${MK_MACHDEP_OPTIMIZATIONS} == no
#   SRCS+=  ${MISRCS}
#
# Machine-independent C is selected instead, and it already exists: of the 80
# machine-dependent .S files libc lists, 74 have a C counterpart in MISRCS by
# construction, and the remaining 6 are ifunc-dispatched variants (_vsx,
# _zva64, _arch_2_05) of functions that also have C. lib/msun honours the
# same option for its ARCH_SRCS, and lib/libmd for rmd160.
#
# It costs performance on the hand-tuned string and math routines. It buys
# 12,344 lines in libc alone that a new architecture no longer has to write,
# and it is one line rather than a rewrite. tools/asm_removal_plan.py has the
# current figures.
WITHOUT_MACHDEP_OPTIMIZATIONS=YES

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hardening, on every architecture.
#
# src.opts.mk makes most of these amd64-only, or amd64-and-arm64:
#
#   .if ${__T} == "amd64"
#   __DEFAULT_YES_OPTIONS+=SAFESTACK
#   __DEFAULT_YES_OPTIONS+=RETPOLINE
#   .else
#   __DEFAULT_NO_OPTIONS+=SAFESTACK
#
# That is upstream being careful about what it has tested, and it is the same
# shape of problem the kernel configs had: the hardening policy differed per
# architecture for reasons that were about coverage rather than about the
# hardware, and the architectures nobody built quietly got less of it.
#
# bsd.mkopt.mk: an option in __DEFAULT_NO_OPTIONS becomes yes when WITH_FOO is
# defined. So this is a configuration change, not a patch to the logic.
#
# pbsd-arch-matrix.yml builds all six and tools/ci/show_hardening.sh reports
# what each one actually got, so "PBSD hardens every architecture equally" is
# a claim with a build behind it rather than an aspiration.

# No hardware dependency. These are linker and codegen choices and there is no
# architecture where they are meaningless.
WITH_PIE=YES
WITHOUT_RELRO=YES
WITH_BIND_NOW=YES

# SafeStack and CFI, where the runtime exists.
#
# These two are not codegen alone. -fsanitize=safe-stack and -fsanitize=cfi
# make clang link a compiler-rt archive, and lib/libclang_rt/Makefile builds
# one only for amd64, aarch64 and i386 - there is no arm, powerpc or riscv
# subdirectory in it at all. Asking for them elsewhere produces a link error
# in world that nothing in the tree or in the packaged toolchain could
# satisfy.
#
# This file said WITH_SAFESTACK / WITH_CFI unconditionally, on the argument
# that upstream's amd64-only default was about test coverage rather than
# hardware. For RETPOLINE and BRANCH_PROTECTION that argument is right and
# they stay as they are. For these two it was wrong, and matrix run 8 is
# where it showed: show_hardening.sh reported SAFESTACK=yes and CFI=yes on
# arm, faithfully, because this file said so - and no runtime for either
# exists on arm. The matrix builds kernels, which do not link libclang_rt,
# so nothing failed. A world build would have.
#
# So the condition is on the runtime existing, which is a real property, and
# it is written here rather than left to be discovered at link time.
# tools/ci/build_boot_image.sh narrows it further when the external
# toolchain ships no archive either. docs/PORTABILITY.md has the table.
.if ${MACHINE_CPUARCH} == "amd64" || ${MACHINE_CPUARCH} == "aarch64" || \
    ${MACHINE_CPUARCH} == "i386"
WITH_SAFESTACK=YES
WITH_CFI=YES
.else
WITHOUT_SAFESTACK=YES
WITHOUT_CFI=YES
.endif

# arm64 branch protection: BTI plus pointer authentication.
# bsd.opts.mk:91 puts BRANCH_PROTECTION in BROKEN_OPTIONS for anything that is
# not aarch64, so this is safe to state unconditionally - it turns on where the
# hardware exists and stays off everywhere else, which is the correct kind of
# architecture-specific. bsd.lib.mk:115 emits -mbranch-protection=standard.
WITH_BRANCH_PROTECTION=YES

# Already on by default, and listed here so it is not mistaken for missing:
#   MK_SSP               bsd.opts.mk __DEFAULT_YES_OPTIONS. Brings
#                        -fstack-protector-strong, -fstack-clash-protection
#                        where the compiler supports it (bsd.sys.mk:312), and
#                        FORTIFY_SOURCE=2.
#   MK_REPRODUCIBLE_BUILD  also default yes.

# RETPOLINE is deliberately NOT set here. It is -mretpoline, an x86
# microarchitectural mitigation for Spectre v2; clang rejects it on other
# targets, and the equivalent elsewhere is different hardware (arm64's BTI and
# pointer authentication, which want MK_BRANCH_PROTECTION - an option this
# tree does not have yet). Leaving it architecture-conditional is correct;
# leaving SafeStack architecture-conditional was not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not built into a shipped image.
#
# Each of these is a subsystem the build can leave out entirely. Reducing by
# configuration rather than by deleting files is the rule this tree learned
# the hard way: the flat import pruned architectures and prebuilt driver
# blobs out of the source, and every one of those cost a build run to find.
# A WITHOUT_ line is reversible, reviewable, and cannot make the tree
# unmergeable with upstream.
#
# Sizes are tracked files under hbsd/src, for scale rather than precision.

WITHOUT_TESTS=YES		# tests 2,400 + netbsd-tests 1,945 + googletest 247
WITHOUT_GAMES=YES
WITHOUT_SHAREDOCS=YES		# share/doc, 428
WITHOUT_HTML=YES
WITHOUT_EXAMPLES=YES
WITHOUT_OFED=YES		# InfiniBand; no target PBSD builds has it

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT set here, and why.
#
# WITHOUT_KERBEROS / WITHOUT_MITKRB5 would remove crypto/krb5 and kerberos5,
# 2,179 files, and for a hardened system there is a real argument that a
# network authentication stack nobody uses is attack surface. But it is
# functionality, not fat: turning it off changes what PBSD can do, not just
# what it ships. That is a product decision and it is not made here.
#
# WITHOUT_TESTS above is not the same kind of choice - it removes the test
# suite from the *image*, and CI still builds with tests enabled, which is
# where the suite is meant to run.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
#
# src.conf.pbsd with RELRO off and NOTHING else changed. It is the last
# single-variable control in the /sbin/init bisect, and it is the one the
# evidence points at.
#
# Runs 43 (SafeStack off), 45 (BIND_NOW off) and 48 (SRCCONF empty - all of
# PBSD's options given up at once) all stop at the SAME instruction:
#
#   rip 0x25653768fd0, load base 0x25653763000  ->  ELF vaddr 0x5fd0
#   nearest preceding symbol: _rtld+0x10
#   two executable mappings, four samples at one rip and one rsp
#
# Two executable mappings is the binary and ld-elf.so.1 and nothing else: the
# rtld has not mapped a single shared library. It is stuck relocating itself,
# which is why a STATIC /rescue/init gets past it.
#
# RELRO is the only option that was ON in every one of those runs, and run 48
# is why: src.opts.mk:59,68,170 puts RELRO and BIND_NOW in
# __DEFAULT_YES_OPTIONS, so "FreeBSD's defaults" is not "no hardening" - it
# still has both. Run 48 therefore exonerates WITHOUT_MACHDEP_OPTIMIZATIONS
# (it tested the OPPOSITE value and hung identically, so the assembly removal
# and ifunc dispatch are not it), PIE, SafeStack, CFI and BRANCH_PROTECTION -
# and leaves RELRO exactly where src.conf.pbsd-nobindnow's own closing note
# left it:
#
#   "If it stays silent, it is RELRO, and this file is the control."
#
# It stayed silent.
#
# The mechanism, if this is it: -z relro alone is PARTIAL relro and leaves
# .got.plt writable; with -z now it is FULL relro, and .got.plt folds into a
# .got the linker marks read-only. ld-elf.so.1 relocates ITSELF before
# anything exists to do it for it, and a write to a page just made read-only
# faults with no handler installed - which from the kernel debugger is a spin
# at a fixed rip with a fixed rsp.
#
# If /sbin/init talks under this file, the fix is to exempt the rtld the way
# libexec/rtld-elf/Makefile:11 and :123 already exempt CFI and PIE, not to
# give up RELRO for the whole system.
#
# If it stays silent under this file too, then no build option is
# responsible, the six-option bisect is finished with nothing to show, and
# the next thing to suspect is this tree's own libc - the generic string
# functions rtld-libc/Makefile.inc:43-47 pulls straight out of
# lib/libc/string, which is code PBSD has changed.
